## =====================================================================
## PCIe-6351 loopback sweep - NI-DAQmx, analog out into analog in
## =====================================================================
##
## Drives ao0 from 0 to +10 V in 1 V steps and reads ai0 at every step.
## With ao0 wired to ai0 it is self-checking: trace N must read N-1 volts,
## so a wrong number is obvious without a meter.
##
##   >>> WIRE AO 0 TO AI 0 with a BNC cable before running this. <<<
##
##   >>> AND CHECK NOTHING ELSE IS ON ao0. This drives a real +10 V. A
##   >>> galvo, piezo or amplifier may not survive it. If something is
##   >>> connected, change 'scan' and 'limits' to a safe span first.
##
## Without the loopback cable it still runs and still proves the scan
## mechanics, but ai0 floats and every trace reads near zero.
##
## Run it in this order:
##
##   pydaq list                              does pscan see the card?
##   pscan --dry-run examples/bringup_6351.con
##   pscan --timer   examples/bringup_6351.con
##
## --timer is half the point of this file: it breaks each trace down so
## you can see what a committed DAQmx task really costs per coordinate on
## this kernel, instead of taking an estimate for it.
##
## Expected output: bringup_6351_a2d1_1f.d
##   11 coordinates x 1 count x 1000 samples x 4 bytes = 44000 bytes
##
## Check it without leaving the shell - the mean of each trace should
## climb 0, 1, 2 ... 10:
##
##   python3 -c "
##   import numpy as np
##   d = np.fromfile('bringup_6351_a2d1_1f.d', dtype=np.float32).reshape(-1, 1000)
##   for i, t in enumerate(d):
##       print(f'{i:3d}  set {i:5.1f} V   read {t.mean():+8.4f} V   sd {t.std():.5f}')
##   "
##
## Reload in MATLAB exactly as any other .d file. The backend makes no
## difference to the format.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
## One trace per coordinate. Raise this to average at a point.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
action count
count 1
end

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
## The output axis: ao0 over 0..10 V in 1 V steps, 11 points.
##
## 'device Dev1' is whatever 'pydaq list' reported - NI assigns that name,
## it is not a /dev node. Drop the line to auto-detect, which works as
## long as only one card can serve the block.
##
## 'settle 0.01' is generous here. A DAC settles in microseconds and there
## is no load on a loopback cable, so this is nearly all margin; it is
## left visible because a real mirror or piezo needs a real number, found
## by raising it until the trace stops changing.
##
## 'restore' returns ao0 to 0 V when the sweep ends rather than leaving it
## parked at +10 V. On this backend an output survives the process, so
## without it the card would still be driving 10 V afterwards.
##
## Axis 98, not the default 99: 99 prints as "Z/piezo" in the log, which
## would be misleading here, and leaving it free means the real piezo can
## be added later as the inner axis without renumbering this one.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
action d2a
axis 98
backend daqmx
device Dev1
channel 0
units volts
scan 0.0 10.0 1.0
limits 0.0 10.0
settle 0.01
restore
end

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
## The input: 1000 samples at 10 kHz off ai0, at every coordinate.
##
## 'v_min'/'v_max' rather than 'range <index>': the volts resolve to the
## narrowest range the card offers that contains them, and still mean the
## same thing if this config is ever pointed at the old comedi card.
## 0..10 V resolves to +/-10 V, the only range that holds it.
##
## 'terminal rse' is single-ended against ground, which is what a BNC
## loopback gives you. Use 'diff' for a differentially wired photodiode -
## on a 6351 that halves the channel count, ai0 pairing with ai8.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
action a2d
channel 0
backend daqmx
device Dev1
v_min 0.0
v_max 10.0
terminal rse
sample_rate 10000
n_samples 1000
end
